

what is Dynamic Block in terraform please explain for devops enginner with real time example on it

In Terraform, a Dynamic Block is used when you need to generate nested configuration blocks dynamically using loops or conditions.

This is very useful in real DevOps infrastructure, especially in AWS resources where a resource requires repeating nested blocks (like security group rules, EBS volumes, listeners, etc.).

Instead of writing the same block many times manually, dynamic blocks create them automatically from a list/map variable.

1. Why Dynamic Blocks are Needed (Real DevOps Problem)
Suppose you are creating an AWS Security Group.

A security group may contain multiple ingress rules.

Example rules:

Allow SSH (22)

Allow HTTP (80)

Allow HTTPS (443)

Normally you write:

resource "aws_security_group" "web_sg" {
  name = "web-sg"

  ingress {
    from_port   = 22
    to_port     = 22
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["10.0.0.0/16"]
  }

  ingress {
    from_port   = 80
    to_port     = 80
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["10.0.0.0/16"]
  }

  ingress {
    from_port   = 443
    to_port     = 443
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["10.0.0.0/16"]
  }
}
But in real production, ports may change per environment.

Example:

Environment	Ports
dev	22, 8080
stage	22, 80
prod	22, 80, 443
So we cannot hardcode ingress blocks.

This is where Dynamic Blocks help.

2. What is Dynamic Block
A dynamic block allows Terraform to generate nested blocks automatically from a list or map.

Syntax:

dynamic "<BLOCK_NAME>" {
  for_each = <collection>

  content {
     # block configuration
  }
}
3. Real AWS DevOps Example (Security Group)
Step 1 — Define Ports Variable
variable "ports" {
  default = [22, 80, 443]
}
Step 2 — Use Dynamic Block
resource "aws_security_group" "web_sg" {
  name = "web-sg"

  dynamic "ingress" {

    for_each = var.ports

    content {
      from_port   = ingress.value
      to_port     = ingress.value
      protocol    = "tcp"
      cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
    }

  }

}
4. How Terraform Processes This
Terraform reads:

var.ports = [22,80,443]
Then generates automatically:

ingress {
  from_port = 22
  to_port   = 22
}

ingress {
  from_port = 80
  to_port   = 80
}

ingress {
  from_port = 443
  to_port   = 443
}
So dynamic block = loop for nested block.

5. Understanding Each Option (Important)
dynamic "ingress"
This tells Terraform:

Create multiple ingress blocks dynamically
for_each
for_each = var.ports
Loop through:

[22,80,443]
ingress.value
Inside dynamic block Terraform automatically creates a temporary object:

ingress.key
ingress.value
Example iteration:

Loop	ingress.value
1	22
2	80
3	443
content block
Defines the actual nested block content.

content {
 from_port = ingress.value
}
6. Dynamic Block with Complex Object (Real Production Example)
Real DevOps engineers rarely pass just ports.

They pass full rule objects.

Example:

variable "sg_rules" {

  default = [
    {
      port = 22
      cidr = "10.0.0.0/16"
    },
    {
      port = 80
      cidr = "0.0.0.0/0"
    }
  ]

}
Dynamic Block
resource "aws_security_group" "web_sg" {

  name = "web-sg"

  dynamic "ingress" {

    for_each = var.sg_rules

    content {

      from_port   = ingress.value.port
      to_port     = ingress.value.port
      protocol    = "tcp"
      cidr_blocks = [ingress.value.cidr]

    }

  }

}
7. Output Generated
Terraform automatically generates:

ingress {
  from_port = 22
  cidr_blocks = ["10.0.0.0/16"]
}

ingress {
  from_port = 80
  cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
}
