I'll show you a complete real-time DevOps example using Dynamic Blocks in AWS that engineers actually use in production.

A very common real scenario is creating a Security Group where rules change per environment (dev/qa/prod).

Instead of writing multiple ingress rules manually, we pass rules as variables and generate them dynamically.

Real Production Example
Dynamic Security Group Rules in AWS
Scenario
You are building a Terraform module for EC2 web servers.

Different environments need different ports:

Environment	Allowed Ports
dev	22, 8080
qa	22, 80
prod	22, 80, 443
Instead of writing many ingress {} blocks manually, we use dynamic blocks.

Project Structure (Real DevOps Repo)
terraform-project/
│
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── terraform.tfvars
└── outputs.tf
1️⃣ variables.tf
Define security group rules as objects.

variable "sg_rules" {

  description = "Security group ingress rules"

  type = list(object({
    port        = number
    description = string
    cidr        = string
  }))

}
This variable allows DevOps teams to add/remove rules easily.

2️⃣ terraform.tfvars
Example rules (production environment).

sg_rules = [

  {
    port        = 22
    description = "SSH Access"
    cidr        = "10.0.0.0/16"
  },

  {
    port        = 80
    description = "HTTP Access"
    cidr        = "0.0.0.0/0"
  },

  {
    port        = 443
    description = "HTTPS Access"
    cidr        = "0.0.0.0/0"
  }

]
3️⃣ main.tf
Now we create the AWS Security Group using a Dynamic Block.

provider "aws" {
  region = "ap-south-1"
}

resource "aws_security_group" "web_sg" {

  name        = "web-server-sg"
  description = "Security group for web servers"

  dynamic "ingress" {

    for_each = var.sg_rules

    content {

      description = ingress.value.description
      from_port   = ingress.value.port
      to_port     = ingress.value.port
      protocol    = "tcp"
      cidr_blocks = [ingress.value.cidr]

    }

  }

  egress {

    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]

  }

}
4️⃣ What Terraform Actually Creates
Terraform reads:

var.sg_rules
and dynamically generates:

ingress {
 description = "SSH Access"
 from_port   = 22
 to_port     = 22
}

ingress {
 description = "HTTP Access"
 from_port   = 80
 to_port     = 80
}

ingress {
 description = "HTTPS Access"
 from_port   = 443
 to_port     = 443
}
5️⃣ Terraform Execution
Run commands:

terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply
Terraform will create:

AWS Security Group
   ├── SSH rule
   ├── HTTP rule
